# Bounded type parameters

## `<T extends Bound>` — "T is-a Bound"

```java
static <T extends Comparable<T>> T max(List<T> list) {
    T best = list.get(0);
    for (T x : list)
        if (x.compareTo(best) > 0) best = x;   // legal — T has compareTo
    return best;
}
```

The bound is both the **promise** (compiler allows `compareTo`) and the **requirement** (caller's type must satisfy it).

## Always `extends` — even for interfaces

`<T extends Number>` · `<T extends Closeable>` — reads "T is a …", constrains the type argument.

## Multiple bounds — `&`

```java
static <T extends Number & Comparable<T>> T clamp(T v, T lo, T hi)
```

At most one class, first; rest interfaces.

## Recursive: `T extends Comparable<T>`

"T comparable **to its own type**" — the canonical `max`/`sort` shape; stops apples vs oranges.

**Bounds ≠ flexibility:** `List<Number>` still won't take a `List<Integer>` → **wildcards** next.
